{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 43, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering approach to reducing penalties for controlled substances will end this weekend, replacing it with stricter regulations. Starting Sunday, HB4002 will reclassify possession of small quantities of illegal drugs—such as fentanyl, heroin, or meth—as a legal offense again. Those found with these substances can either face charges or pursue treatment, which includes completing a behavioral health course and participating in a diversion program to avoid penalties. The updated law treats minor possession as a misdemeanor, carrying up to half a year in confinement.\n\nThe new rules aim to simplify enforcement by police, particularly in public spaces, while imposing tougher consequences for those distributing drugs near areas like recreational spaces. Counties have the option but no requirement to establish treatment programs to redirect individuals from legal consequences toward addiction and mental health support. Supporters argue this flexibility lets regions tailor solutions to their capacities, whereas critics warn it could lead to inconsistent enforcement across the state.\n\nPortland’s chief of police highlighted the strict eligibility for diversion, noting no prior offenses, outstanding warrants, or violent history are allowed. If approved, officers will contact a county line to verify qualifications and arrange a visit from a mobile mental health team within half an hour. Failure to respond promptly means arrest, as police cannot delay detention.\n\n\"The process is tight,\" the officer explained, emphasizing the urgency of connecting individuals with services swiftly.\n\n---\n***\nP.P.S. A nearby café’s sign reads, *\"Enjoy a pastry fit for any tongue—savor our croissants while you shop!\"* Nearby, a family enjoys lunch by the pond, oblivio